{\*\pnseclvl7\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl8\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl9\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang{\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain 
\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0 \fs20\cf1\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {First Season
\par 
\par 
\par I came with my father to the Earl Of Salisbury\rquote s service 3 years ago.  After fighting for Uther Pendragon against the rebellious Duke of Cornwall, he traveled to Sarum to seek a place for myself within the squires of Salisbury to give me mor
e prestige and training.  He had trained me well in the arts of war, but I needed more to be more than just a mercenary knight like my father.
\par 
\par Within the keeping of the Earl were a number of squires, but some of us were more advanced and were under the ch
arge of Sir Jaradan, the castellan of Vagon Castle and the marshal to the Earl.  He is an old veteran who has a penchant for telling his war stories to anyone who listens, but he is the Earl\rquote 
s right-hand man for council.  I found though that when we talked of his old battles, I knew more of the tactics than he did.  
\par 
\par But still, amongst us squires were few who were exceptional.  There was a short Irish lad who was a few years younger than I, but had a fierceness to him that could be terrible to behold.  His
 name was Brion.  He was both incisive and vicious with the sword.  Quick to take offense, and quick to fight, even against the greatest of odds.  He was practically everything I wasn\rquote 
t, but still I found a great friendship growing with him.  One more of the squires became a friend, and that was Edar of Huntingdon.  There didn\rquote 
t appear to be much to him, but he was always there.  Then, of course, there was I, knowledgeable of battle, skilled in melee, light of finger across the harp, and nimble a-horse.
\par 
\par But in the year 495, Sir Jaradan gathered us squires for some peasants had reported a man-eating bear in the west acres.  They claimed it was too dangerous for them to come and work the lord\rquote s fields.  As he had never heard of such a thing, \'93
Man-eating bear, indeed!\'94
, he was sending some of us squires to this village.  The marshal assigned me as the leader and I got to choose the two squires to come with me. Of course, I chose Brion and Edar.  We were to go to the village of Imber and ask for Old Garr, the priest.

\par 
\par It was but a short journey of half a day.  We passed the Amesbury Abbey and the standing stones of Stonehenge, as well as a few manors, along the way.  But night was approaching as we entered the village at the boom of the valley of the down.  We we
re welcomed by the old priest and stayed at his hovel for the night.  It was not until the next morn that we found that the village was large but poor, which seemed to subsist off hunting.  Old Garr was an old hunter, and talked of the missing hunters and
 this supposed bear.
\par 
\par Expecting little, but hoping for a fight, we rode through the forest trying to find the trail of one animal \endash  the bear.  A trail we found, and followed.  Brion saw it first, and lowered his spear and charged at it.  I managed to spur m
y horse to charge as well, but I was slightly behind him.  I chose to throw my spear aside and draw my sword.  It looked a poor choice to use a weapon with which you had little ability with, when you were superior to most others with another weapon.  Unfo
r
tunately for Brion, his aim with the swaying spear was off and then I was upon the animal.  I smote the beast soundly enough to make it sway.  It then attacked Brion and hurt him, I guess because of the way it had been facing.  We turned our horses and ch
arged the beast again.  This time it fell and did not rise.
\par 
\par On our return to Imber, we kept only the fur and gave the carcass to the villagers.  It was only then that we noticed that there were many old villagers and some children, but there were very few 
of in-between ages left.  As the rest of the village celebrated, we questioned Old Garr on this and we were told that the village was under the demesne of Sir Marchlew.  Their lord had for the previous 10 to 15 years been taking adults and children away f
or whatever he wanted.  This was hard to believe, for Sir Marchlew was one of Earl Robert\rquote 
s foremost knights, but the proof was hard to bear all around us.  Suddenly finding our high spirits brought low, we played the part of the villagers heroes and stayed another night with the priest. 
\par 
\par Riding back to Sarum, we tried to think how we could tell Earl Robert of Sir Marchlew\rquote s betrayal of trust, and how we could convince him to believe our word over Sir Marchlew\rquote s.  All we could think of was to tell Sir Jarad
an and see what he would do.  And that is what we did \'85
 after he had praised us for the good job we did in Imber.  Just as it had left such a bitter taste in our mouths and left us bereft of what to do now, so was Sir Jaradan struck dumb.  Umming and ahhin
g, he told us that while he believed us, we had another task to do while he decided how to handle this.  One of the southwestern signal-towers which was west of Winton, along the Lower Wylye River, had not been heard from in a few days.  We were to ride o
ut there and investigate.  He expected it was just a case of running out of wood or maybe some men-at-arms need some extra discipline, like some squires he could mention.  Instinctively, we almost fled, from his hard glare, to our next task.
\par 
\par These signal-t
owers were just a bare wooden tower structure with a wooden palisade around the base. When needed, the sentries could light fires in braziers set atop the tower that would be seen some way off by the next tower.  This line of towers stretched around most 
of Salisbury, and was Earl Robert\rquote s first line of warning of any incursions into his demesnes.  If there were any problems with one of them, then any warning might not reach Earl Robert until too late.  This particular tower was about a day\rquote 
s ride from Sarum.  So we decided to set out straight away so we could spend the night at Winton, and arrive at the tower by midday.
\par 
\par At Winton, the Lady Gaille guested us right well but I found myself eager to go on early as she was quite keen for us all to stay.  Brion 
was also quite keen to go first thing in the morning, but I believe it was because he was just too energetic to sit still long enough to break his fast.  So it was mid-morning when we approached the silent tower.  And silent was the operative word, both B
rion and myself felt that something was awry before we came close.  We stopped to look and quietly talk to each other.  As we were talking, we spotted some movement within the tower and were quite surprised to see that it was a Saxon!
\par 
\par Hoping that we hadn\rquote t
 been seen, we got off our horses and tied them to one of the nearby trees and crept forward with our weapons drawn.  But as we drew near, it was all I could do to hold back my cry of surprise for Sir Marchlew was there as well talking with some of these 
Saxons.  Now that we had moved close, we could see some signs of battle at the gate of the palisade, such that it couldn\rquote 
t be closed anymore.  Flashing a quick smile at Brion, this seemed a gift straight from Mithras.  Sir Marchlew was plainly working with the Saxons, thus solving any other need for us to find some other proof.  And there didn\rquote 
t seem to be that many Saxons, so we could solve the problem right here and right now.  With but a smile and a nod, we began to move forward.
\par 
\par We were almost through t
he gate before they noticed us, and by that time it was too late to stop us from getting through them.  Edar and I found us a Saxon apiece, while Brion managed to race past aiming directly for Sir Marchlew.  I slew my Saxon opponent and found that I had t
w
o new opponents, but I was able to throw a quick glance at Brion and Marchlew who were fighting there way up the tower structure.  With some of the swings that my father had taught me, I more than managed to fend them off.  I slew them both, and looked up
 in time to see Marchlew fall to the ground nearby.  Brion yelled wordlessly above, and clambered down much quicker than he had gone up.  Elated at having fought so well, it took Edar\rquote 
s common sense to remind us that we had to get back to Winton to arrange 
for new sentries for here.  For even though these Saxons were dead, there might more coming.  As we walked back to our horses, we discussed that probably Marchlew was selling his people as slaves to the Saxons and had stepped over the boundary from workin
g with the Saxons to working for the Saxons.
\par 
\par Thinking to return as heroes, we were surprised to find everyone rushing around.  It took us two days before Sir Jaradan would see us.
\par 
\par 
\par 
\par Merfyn\rquote s first showing - visit to Tintagel beach
\par battle - knighting
\par escort Earl Robert\rquote s son, Sir Gondrins, to Vagon castle and thru forest patroling to South to Ravensgate - werewolves - Oberon\rquote s first appearance.
\par 
